+// PlayStation 5: in-between frames keep the step's shadow maps.
+//
+// At 60 fps every step of the game is drawn twice (frame interpolation: interp.cpp), each pass in
+// full, and more than half of a pass's draws fill the shadow maps: three 1024x1024 slices of one
+// depth array (the cascades), drawn with no colour target. Two full passes of a view across the
+// first island do not fit in a thirtieth of a second on the render thread.
+//
+// So on an in-between pass the draws into that depth array, and its clears (surfaces.cpp), are
+// left out: the maps of the step's own frame stay, 16 ms old for what moved. The game applies its
+// shadows in one full-screen draw, the sun shadow mask, whose uniform block holds for each cascade
+// the matrix from view space to that cascade's map: bias * light * inverseView. The in-between
+// frame has another camera, and the game fits the cascades to the camera, so its own three
+// matrices belong to maps that were not drawn (used with the kept maps, whole surfaces went dark
+// whenever the camera turned: the first attempt, 2026-10-11). Here the light matrices are taken
+// from the shadow-map draws of the step's frame (their uniform block 2), checked against that
+// frame's mask, and the in-between frame's mask gets bias * thatLight * itsOwnInverseView.
+// A step whose check fails draws its shadow maps in the in-between frame as before.
